# Solution — GIAM 6.2 Problem 4

## Problem

For each of the five properties, name a relation that has it and a relation that doesn't.

## Setup

Throughout, $ X $ is a non-empty set and $ R \subseteq X \times X $ is a relation. The five properties:

- **Reflexive.** For every $ x \in X $, $ (x, x) \in R $.
- **Irreflexive.** For every $ x \in X $, $ (x, x) \notin R $.
- **Symmetric.** $ (x, y) \in R \;\Longrightarrow\; (y, x) \in R $.
- **Antisymmetric.** $ \bigl[(x, y) \in R \text{ and } (y, x) \in R\bigr] \;\Longrightarrow\; x = y $.
- **Transitive.** $ \bigl[(x, y) \in R \text{ and } (y, z) \in R\bigr] \;\Longrightarrow\; (x, z) \in R $.

The examples below all live on familiar sets — $ \mathbb{R} $ for the order relations, an arbitrary set for $ = $ and $ \ne $.

## Reflexive

- **Has it.** $ \le $ on $ \mathbb{R} $: $ x \le x $ for every real $ x $.
- **Doesn't.** $ < $ on $ \mathbb{R} $: $ x < x $ is never true, so the diagonal is missing entirely.

Note that "doesn't have reflexivity" here is in fact the stronger property *irreflexive*. The two are not negations of one another: a relation can fail to be reflexive without being irreflexive — for instance, $ \{(1, 1)\} $ on $ \{1, 2\} $ contains the diagonal point at $ 1 $ but not at $ 2 $.

## Irreflexive

- **Has it.** $ < $ on $ \mathbb{R} $: $ x < x $ never holds.
- **Doesn't.** $ \le $ on $ \mathbb{R} $: every diagonal pair $ (x, x) $ lies in the relation.

## Symmetric

- **Has it.** $ = $ on any set: $ x = y \;\Longrightarrow\; y = x $. (Or "$ x $ has the same astrological sign as $ y $" from Problem 2.)
- **Doesn't.** $ < $ on $ \mathbb{R} $: $ 1 < 2 $ but $ \lnot(2 < 1) $.

## Antisymmetric

- **Has it.** $ \le $ on $ \mathbb{R} $: if $ a \le b $ and $ b \le a $, then $ a = b $.
- **Doesn't.** $ \ne $ on any set with at least two elements: pick distinct $ a, b $. Then $ a \ne b $ and $ b \ne a $, yet $ a \ne b $ — the antisymmetric implication fails on this pair.

## Transitive

- **Has it.** $ < $ on $ \mathbb{R} $: $ a < b $ and $ b < c $ give $ a < c $.
- **Doesn't.** "Is a parent of." If Alice is a parent of Bob and Bob is a parent of Carol, then Alice is Carol's *grandparent*, not parent. (A formal version: the relation $ R = \{(1, 2), (2, 3)\} $ on $ \{1, 2, 3\} $ contains $ (1, 2) $ and $ (2, 3) $ but not $ (1, 3) $.)

## Remark — A Compact Cheat Sheet

A handful of canonical relations cover all five properties in both directions. Take any non-empty set $ X $; for $ \le $ and $ < $, take $ X = \mathbb{R} $.

| Relation | Reflexive | Irreflexive | Symmetric | Antisymmetric | Transitive |
| --- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |
| $ = $   | Yes | No  | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| $ \ne $ | No  | Yes | Yes | No  | No  |
| $ \le $ | Yes | No  | No  | Yes | Yes |
| $ < $   | No  | Yes | No  | Yes | Yes |

A few patterns worth noting:

- **Strict vs. weak.** The strict relations ($ <, \ne $) are irreflexive; the weak relations ($ \le, = $) are reflexive. Reflexive and irreflexive form a clean dual axis here, even though in general they are not negations.
- **Equality is the unique all-yes row.** This is no accident: a relation that is symmetric *and* antisymmetric must be a sub-relation of the diagonal, so equality (and its sub-relations) are the only candidates for the all-yes row (cf. Problem 2's remark).
- **$ < $ is antisymmetric vacuously.** No pair $ (a, b) $ has both $ a < b $ and $ b < a $, so the implication's hypothesis is unsatisfiable and the column entry is "Yes" by default — the same trick that made "smarter than" antisymmetric in Problem 1.
- **$ \ne $ is the most lopsided.** It fails three of the five (reflexive, antisymmetric, transitive) yet has the other two (irreflexive, symmetric) — handy when one needs a relation that emphatically isn't an order or an equivalence.
